**Reasoning**: The evaluator charges each distinct directed edge once per partition (evidenced by the Steiner improvement from 1035→693). Two refinements lower that charge: (a) splicing only the broken hop keeps the rest of the shared trunk instead of abandoning it, and (b) comparing the Steiner routing's unique-edge cost against the per-dst Dijkstra baseline and emitting the cheaper one guarantees we never do worse than the incumbent while usually doing better. Compute stays bounded: one Steiner call plus O(dsts) Dijkstras, identical to the parent's budget. Validity is preserved since every appended hop is a real directed edge of `h` with its actual edge data, and every reachable dst gets a full src→dst chain per partition.
